    "pthm-qft-example-formal-scattering-decay": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-example-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "These are conventions for constructed and formal calculations in this page pair. They are not empirical claims, physical postulates, or existence claims about an interacting continuum theory.\n\nWork on a nonempty open coordinate region $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$, with coordinates $x^0=ct,x^1,x^2,x^3$ in metres and $\\partial_a=\\partial/\\partial x^a$ for $a\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$. Use the geometric spacetime metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and the time coordinate $x^0=ct$ in metres. For Clifford algebra and momentum contraction use the separate algebraic metric $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$; a slash or a square of an energy four-vector uses $h$, never $\\eta$.\n\nA physical energy four-vector is $p=(E,c\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys})$, with each component in joules. Write $M=mc^2$ for rest energy in joules and $p^2=h(p,p)=E^2-c^2|\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys}|^2$. For two such vectors, $s=(p_1+p_2)^2$ is in $\\mathrm J^2$. Natural momentum coordinates are the energy-vector components divided by $\\hbar c$, in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$; they are not substituted into SI formulas without restoring this factor. Here $m\\ge0$ is a mass parameter in kilograms, so $\\mu=mc/\\hbar$ is in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. The natural-unit field convention below concerns smooth test fields, not pointwise quantum operators. A real scalar field is a map $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R)$; a Dirac test field is $\\Psi\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb C^4)$. Fix dimensionless matrices $\\gamma^a\\in M_4(\\mathbb C)$ satisfying $\\gamma^a\\gamma^b+\\gamma^b\\gamma^a=2h^{ab}I_4$, and put $\\overline\\Psi=\\Psi^\\dagger\\gamma^0$. Adopt dimensionless action normalizations with kinetic terms $\\tfrac12\\int_U h^{ab}(\\partial_a\\varphi)(\\partial_b\\varphi)\\,d^4x$ and $\\int_U i\\overline\\Psi\\gamma^a\\partial_a\\Psi\\,d^4x$, summing repeated indices from $0$ to $3$. The coefficients are dimensionless and introduce no additional length scale. These conventions assign $[\\varphi]=\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and $[\\Psi]=\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$: the integrands have length degrees $2[\\varphi]-2$ and $2[\\Psi]-1$, while $d^4x$ has degree $4$. No dynamics or continuum existence assertion is adopted.\n\nTake SI $e>0$ in coulombs, $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, $c>0$ in metres per second, $\\mu_0>0$ in $\\mathrm{H\\,m^{-1}}=\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,C^{-2}}$, and $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ in $\\mathrm{F\\,m^{-1}}$. Define\n$$\\alpha=\\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad g=e\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}>0.$$\nHere $g$ is a dimensionless coupling. It is not the metric and not a gyromagnetic factor.\n\nFor gauge notation take $\\phi\\in C^1(U;\\mathbb R)$ in volts and $\\mathbf A\\in C^1(U;\\mathbb R^3)$ in tesla-metres. Write $A_{\\rm lower}=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A)$ for the components of the real one-form $A=\\sum_{a=0}^3 A_a\\,dx^a$, so every $A_a$ is in tesla-metres. Take $\\chi\\in C^2(U;\\mathbb R)$ in webers and a charged test field $\\psi\\in C^1(U;\\mathbb C^N)$, where $N\\ge1$ is fixed. With a constant real charge parameter $q$ in coulombs, define $D_a\\psi=\\partial_a\\psi-iqA_a\\psi/\\hbar$ componentwise. The transformed fields are again $C^1$, with $d\\chi=\\sum_a(\\partial_a\\chi)dx^a$. In operator notation the conventions are\n$$D_a=\\partial_a-\\frac{iqA_a}{\\hbar},\\qquad \\psi\\mapsto e^{iq\\chi/\\hbar}\\psi,\\qquad A\\mapsto A+d\\chi.$$\nFor the electron parameter use $q=-e$. The equivalent natural-unit coordinates are $A_{\\rm nat}=A_{\\rm SI}/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}$ and $q_{\\rm nat}=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$.\n\nFinite-matrix examples act on $\\mathbb C^d$ with the operator norm. For Hilbert spaces, use the first-variable-linear pairing $(u,v)_1=v^\\dagger u$; a displayed coordinate contraction $u^\\dagger v$ is second-variable-linear and equals $\\overline{(u,v)_1}$. Thus norms, traces, and modulus-squared amplitudes agree under conversion, although the unsquared pairing is conjugated. No amplitude is assigned a probability by these conventions.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.4",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-example-observable-types",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the framework-local unit and domain dictionary of\n[[def-qft-framework-units]] and the energy, SI/natural-unit, and finite-matrix\nconventions of [[def-qft-example-data]]. A physical quantity is always typed\nrelative to the selected QFT formulation and its declared model data; its name\nor units alone do not identify it with a quantity in another formulation.\n\nFor the **exact finite-regulator class** used on this page, the state space is\nthe finite-dimensional Hilbert space $\\mathcal H=\\mathbb C^d$ for an integer\n$d\\ge1$. Model inputs include the selected Hermitian Hamiltonian matrices,\ntheir time-dependent Hermitian interaction when present, a dimensionless\ncoupling parameter, an initial state, and an explicitly supplied finite\noutcome measurement. A pure initial state is a vector $\\psi\\in\\mathbb C^d$\nwith $\\|\\psi\\|=1$; a mixed state is a matrix $\\rho=\\rho^*$ with\n$\\langle\\rho v,v\\rangle\\ge0$ for every $v$ and $\\operatorname{tr}\\rho=1$.\nAn exact regulated observable on this finite space is a specified Hermitian\nmatrix $O=O^*$ with a declared physical unit. The matrix and state are model\ndata; the measurement procedure and any physical rule assigning outcomes are\nadditional data. This definition does not say that an operator symbol by\nitself is a detector outcome or probability.\n\nA **finite probability law** on the declared outcome set\n$\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$, $m\\ge1$, is a vector $(p_1,\\ldots,p_m)$ with\n$p_a\\ge0$ and $\\sum_{a=1}^m p_a=1$. It is a mathematical type. Assigning that\nlaw to a particular preparation and measurement is a separate physical\npostulate. A prediction derived from an actually constructed regulated model\nis conditional on those inputs and the adopted rule.\n\nA **formal coefficient record** is a sequence $(c_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ of\nspecified coefficients, sometimes displayed as the symbol\n$\\sum_{n\\ge0}c_n\\lambda^n$. Unless convergence and an evaluation map are\nseparately proved and adopted, the symbol records coefficients only: it is not\nan evaluated function, exact probability, convergent perturbation series, or\nerror bound. A formal coefficient can be assigned a phenomenological meaning\nonly by a separate, model-specific physical adoption with its approximation\nscope stated.\n\nAn **empirical estimate** is a source-reported number obtained by analyzing a\nfinite record of measurement outcomes under the stated preparation, apparatus,\ncalibration, sampling, and analysis conditions. Its reported uncertainty and\nconditions travel with the estimate. It is not the exact model value, and it\ndoes not prove the framework used to interpret it. The 2022 Fan et al. report\nis an example of a source-reported electron magnetic-moment determination;\nthis definition supplies no numerical result, uncertainty reconstruction, or\nnew experimental claim.\n\nPrimitive and derived status is formulation-relative. In a selected regulated\nmodel, the regulator, Hamiltonian data, coupling, initial state, and supplied\nmeasurement are declared inputs; an evolution, amplitude, or probability law\nis derived only when the construction and adopted rule establish it. In a\nformal calculation, the specified masses, couplings, coefficient rules, and\nsubtraction prescription are inputs, while its listed coefficients are\nderived within that formal calculation. An empirical estimate is a reported\ndata-analysis result, not a primitive model parameter or a theorem.\n\nThe following units are examples of types, not identifications of quantities:\na cross section $\\sigma$ is area-valued with SI unit $\\mathrm{m^2}$; a rate\n$\\Gamma$ is inverse-time-valued with unit $\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$; an energy width\n$\\Gamma_E$ has unit joule. Only a separately stated convention may identify\n$\\Gamma_E=\\hbar\\Gamma$. The fine-structure parameter $\\alpha$, a dimensionless\ncoupling $g$, and a gyromagnetic factor $g_s$ are dimensionless but have\ndifferent physical meanings. The name “width,” a common symbol, or matching\ndimensions does not supply an exponential-decay law or an equality between\nthese parameters.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the finite Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], and the separate geometric metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and algebraic contraction $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$ of [[def-qft-example-data]]. Write $p=(E,\\mathbf p)$ for an energy four-vector with components in joules, $M\\geq0$ for a rest-energy parameter in joules, and $E=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf p|^2+M^2}>0$. In the natural-length variables of the Dirac definition, $\\mathbf k=\\mathbf p/(\\hbar c)$, $\\mu=M/(\\hbar c)$, and $\\omega=E/(\\hbar c)$.\n\nLet $\\beta=\\gamma^0$, $\\alpha_j=\\beta\\gamma^j$, $\\Sigma_j=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_j,\\sigma_j)$, and $\\gamma_5=i\\gamma^0\\gamma^1\\gamma^2\\gamma^3$. Define $H_\\pm=\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf p\\pm\\beta M$ and $P_\\pm=(I_4+H_\\pm/E)/2$. Each $P_\\pm$ is a Hermitian rank-two projection. Choose orthonormal bases $\\xi_{\\pm,1},\\xi_{\\pm,2}$ of their ranges and put $u_s=\\sqrt{2E}\\xi_{+,s}$ and $v_s=\\sqrt{2E}\\xi_{-,s}$ for $s=1,2$. With $\\bar z=z^\\dagger\\beta$ and $\\not p=\\gamma^0E-\\sum_j\\gamma^jp_j$, the following hold:\n\n$$\\operatorname{Tr}I_4=4,\\quad \\operatorname{Tr}\\gamma^a=0,\\quad \\operatorname{Tr}(\\gamma^a\\gamma^b)=4h^{ab},\\quad \\operatorname{Tr}(\\gamma^a\\gamma^b\\gamma^c)=0,$$\n\n$$\\operatorname{Tr}(\\gamma^a\\gamma^b\\gamma^c\\gamma^d) =4(h^{ab}h^{cd}-h^{ac}h^{bd}+h^{ad}h^{bc}), \\qquad \\sum_s u_s\\bar u_s=\\not p+M,\\quad \\sum_s v_s\\bar v_s=\\not p-M.$$\n\nThe columns satisfy $(\\not p-M)u_s=0$ and $(\\not p+M)v_s=0$. For all $x,y\\in\\mathbb C^4$, complex conjugation of a Dirac current is $\\overline{\\bar x\\gamma^a y}=\\bar y\\gamma^a x$. The Countable Choice hypothesis written in the current draft [[def-qft-dirac-projections]] is retained as a supplier assumption; this finite-dimensional proof makes no additional choice use.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], and use [[def-qft-example-data]] and [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. Write\n$$\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M}=\\{(E,\\mathbf q)\\in(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R^3:E=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf q|^2+\\mathsf M^2}\\}$$\nfor the positive shell in that Definition, and $C_+=\\{(E,\\mathbf q)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3:E\\geq|\\mathbf q|\\}$ for the closed future cone of $h=(+,-,-,-)$. A proper orthochronous linear $h$-Lorentz map is a real linear $\\Lambda$ with $\\Lambda^Th\\Lambda=h$, $\\det\\Lambda=1$, and $\\Lambda(C_+)=C_+$. For energy four-vectors on these positive shells, the shell measure $\\nu_{\\mathsf M}$ defined there is invariant under every such map. Let $\\mathsf M_1,\\mathsf M_2\\geq0$, let $P$ be future timelike, meaning $P^0>0$ and $h(P,P)>0$, set $s=h(P,P)>(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2$, and write $E_i(r)=\\sqrt{r^2+\\mathsf M_i^2}$. Let $f$ be a nonnegative Borel function on $\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_1}\\times\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_2}$. For a proper orthochronous $h$-Lorentz map $\\Lambda_P$ with $\\Lambda_P P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$, define $\\Phi_{2,P}$ as the pushforward of the angular measure $\\Phi_2$ from that Definition under\n$$\\mathbf n\\longmapsto\\left(\\Lambda_P^{-1}(E_1^*,k_*\\mathbf n),\\Lambda_P^{-1}(E_2^*,-k_*\\mathbf n)\\right),$$\nwhere $E_i^*$ and $k_*$ are from [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. The proof below shows that $\\Phi_{2,P}$ is independent of the choice of $\\Lambda_P$; for $P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$ it is the pushforward $(\\Psi_2)_\\#\\Phi_2$ under the center-of-momentum map $\\Psi_2$ defined there. The standard two-body on-shell integral\n$$(2\\pi)^4\\int d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_1}(k_1)d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_2}(k_2)\\, \\delta^{(4)}(P-k_1-k_2)\\,f(k_1,k_2)$$\nis interpreted by first evaluating the spatial delta in $k_2$, then the one-dimensional energy delta by the increasing radial change of variable $r\\mapsto E_1(r)+E_2(r)$. Explicitly, for each fixed angular direction and nonnegative Borel radial function $g:(0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty]$, the radial action after spatial evaluation is $\\int_0^\\infty g(r)\\delta(\\sqrt{s}-E_1(r)-E_2(r))\\,dr=g(k_*)/(d(E_1+E_2)/dr|_{k_*})$; this is the pushforward evaluation under that $C^1$ diffeomorphism, not a product of distributions. It equals $\\int f\\,d\\Phi_{2,P}$. The threshold root is $k_*$ in that Definition, and\n$$\\Phi_{2,P}(\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_1}\\times\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_2})=\\Phi_2(S^2)=\\frac{k_*}{4\\pi\\sqrt{s}}.$$\nFor equal rest energies $\\mathsf M_1=\\mathsf M_2=\\mathsf M$, set $\\beta=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/s}$; then $k_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta/2$ and $\\Phi_{2,P}(\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M}\\times\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M})=\\Phi_2(S^2)=\\beta/(8\\pi)$. For the scattering flux identities, take incoming $p_i=(E_i,\\mathbf p_i)\\in\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_{i,\\mathrm{in}}}$, where $\\mathsf M_{i,\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$, and require $p_1+p_2=P$. A center-of-momentum map is a proper orthochronous $h$-Lorentz map $\\Lambda$ with $\\Lambda P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$; write $\\Lambda p_1=(E_1^*,\\mathbf p_1^*)$ and define $p_*=|\\mathbf p_1^*|$. The value is independent of the choice of $\\Lambda$, since two such maps differ by a spatial rotation. Their invariant flux is $F=4p_*\\sqrt{s}$. For equal incoming rest energies $\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$ with $s\\geq4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2$, define $\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2/s}$; then $p_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}/2$ and $F=2s\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}$. For finite nonempty incoming and outgoing label sets, normalized initial weights, and the Borel amplitude family of the Definition, whenever $F>0$ the cross-section coefficient map gives\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega} =\\frac{(\\hbar c)^2}{64\\pi^2s}\\, \\frac{k_*}{p_*}\\, \\frac{\\overline{|\\mathcal A|^2}}{S_f}.$$\nFor a parent at rest, represented by $P_0=(\\mathsf M_0,\\mathbf0)$ with $\\mathsf M_0>2\\mathsf M$, and two equal-rest-energy daughters on $\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M}$ with total four-vector $P_0$ and constant amplitude $G_E$ in joules, the rate coefficient is $|G_E|^2\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/\\mathsf M_0^2}/(32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0)$ when the daughters are identical. This is a coefficient calculation, not a claim of exponential survival.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-qft-example-scalar-tree-coefficients",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m\\geq0$ be a scalar rest energy in joules, and let $p_1,p_2,k_1,k_2$ be the incoming and outgoing scattering vectors on the positive $m$-shell of [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]], so each $(E,\\mathbf q)$ satisfies $E=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf q|^2+m^2}>0$. Require $p_1+p_2=k_1+k_2=P$ and $s=h(P,P)>4m^2$, with $h$ as in [[def-qft-example-data]]. Evaluate the coefficient map in a center-of-momentum frame $\\Lambda P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$ as defined in [[lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space]]. For one scalar initial label of weight $1$ and one specified final channel, take the dimensionless real quartic tree amplitude $\\mathcal A=-\\lambda$. The coefficient map of [[lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space]] gives\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega} =\\frac{\\lambda^2(\\hbar c)^2}{128\\pi^2s}, \\qquad \\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}=\\frac{\\lambda^2(\\hbar c)^2}{32\\pi s}$$\nfor identical final scalars integrated over the full labeled sphere. For distinguishable final scalars the two coefficients are twice these values.\n\nFor a parent at rest, $P_0=(\\mathsf M_0,\\mathbf0)$ with energy $\\mathsf M_0>2m$, decaying through a specified single channel to two identical scalars on the positive $m$-shell whose total four-vector is $P_0$, under a constant amplitude $G_E$ in joules, the rate coefficient is\n$$\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{coeff}} =\\frac{|G_E|^2}{32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0} \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4m^2}{\\mathsf M_0^2}}.$$\nThis result concerns the defined formal coefficients. It does not assert an exact exponential survival law.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-qft-example-formal-observable-map",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice, $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ [[def-countable-choice]], as required by the cited mathematical phase-space map.\n\nFor a specifically named finite tree calculation, adopt its selected formal\ndiagram coefficient as a **leading perturbative coefficient** with a stated\nphenomenological reading. For QED, use the vertex and free-line conventions\nof the current draft supplier\n[[post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients]]. A particle of natural charge $q_n$ has\nvertex $iq_n\\gamma^a$; the electron has $q_n=-e_n$ and vertex\n$-ie_n\\gamma^a$. The rational fermion line is\n$$ i\\frac{\\not p+\\mu}{h(p,p)-\\mu^2}, $$\nfor $\\mu>0$ and off-shell $p$ with $h(p,p)-\\mu^2\\ne0$. In Feynman gauge\nthe auxiliary photon line is\n$$ -i\\frac{h_{ab}}{h(k,k)+i0}=i\\frac{\\eta_{ab}}{h(k,k)+i0}. $$\nFor a finite matrix kernel the coefficientwise inverse in\n[[post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients]] is part of the formal convention and\nasserts no convergence. If a time-ordered distribution is used, use the\nactual free carrier specified there; that identification is conditional on\n[[def-countable-choice]]. The displayed pole notation does not define loop\nproducts. In the pair-production channel below the selected muon also has\nmodel charge $-e$, and only the photon line is internal.\n\nFor declared incoming spin probabilities, sum over declared final spin\nlabels, and a specified formal amplitude $\\mathcal A$, adopt the\ncross-section coefficient map of\n[[lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space]]. Its scattering inputs are\npositive-shell energy vectors $p_i=(E_i,\\mathbf p_i)$ with\n$E_i=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf p_i|^2+\\mathsf M_{i,\\rm in}^2}>0$,\n$\\mathsf M_{i,\\rm in}\\ge0$, and common future-timelike total\n$P=p_1+p_2=k_1+k_2$. The outgoing $k_j$ are on their specified positive\nshells. Require $s=h(P,P)>(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2$ for the outgoing\nrest energies and positive incoming flux $F>0$, precisely the supplier's\nadmissible scattering domain. In the center-of-momentum frame it\nuses the invariant incoming flux $F$, the two-body measure $d\\Phi_2$, and the\nfinal-state factor $S_f=2$ for identical particles or $S_f=1$ for\ndistinguishable particles, integrated over the full labeled sphere. When\nthis coefficient is read phenomenologically, the initial preparation,\nchannel, threshold, and approximation are part of the model statement. No\nuniversal error estimate for omitted terms is implied.\n\nFor the scalar examples, separately adopt the constant formal amplitudes\n$\\mathcal A=-\\lambda$ for the quartic contact channel and $\\mathcal G=G_E$\nfor the cubic parent decay, with the units and external normalization stated\nat each use. The scalar scattering inputs lie on their stated positive\nshells with a common total and the same strict threshold and positive-flux\nconditions. For decay, the parent is $P_0=(\\mathsf M_0,\\mathbf0)$,\n$\\mathsf M_0>\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2$, and its two positive-shell daughters\nsum to $P_0$. Their coefficient map is the one in\n[[lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space]]. If a rate $\\Gamma$ is reported as\nan energy width, the convention is explicitly $\\Gamma_E:=\\hbar\\Gamma$; it\ndoes not supply a survival law.\n\nThe readings above do not identify a formal coefficient with an exact\ncontinuum cross section, probability, decay rate, or measured estimate. In\nparticular they do not construct an interacting continuum $S$-matrix or LSZ\nlimit, establish convergence of a perturbation series, bound loop or\nsoft-radiation effects, specify beam or detector cuts, resolve threshold\nresummation, or reproduce the statistical analysis of an experiment. A\ncomparison with observations would require the actual apparatus, sampling,\ncalibration, uncertainty, and radiative-inclusiveness conditions. The\nreported electron magnetic-moment measurements, including the Fan et al.\nreport listed in the source references, remain source-reported\nempirical results and are not premises of this adoption.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A3",
          "source": "post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt the following coefficient convention for finite formal QED bookkeeping in\nthe Minkowski, units, and Clifford conventions of\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. In particular,\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $h=-\\eta$,\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=2h^{ab}I_4$, and\n$\\not p:=\\gamma^a h_{ab}p^b$. The inverse-length mass is\n$\\mu=m_{\\rm rest,SI}c/\\hbar$ and a declared physical charge $q_{\\rm SI}$ has\ndimensionless value $q_{\\rm n}=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$.\n\nA **formal coefficient graph** here is a finite directed graph of interaction\nvertices and internal lines with a momentum covector assigned to each line and\nmomentum conservation imposed at each vertex. It records a coefficient at a\nfixed finite or formal order; it is not, by this definition, a convergent\namplitude or an operator on a continuum interacting Hilbert space. For a Dirac\nspecies of dimensionless charge $g$, use $D_a=\\partial_a-ig a_a$ and adopt the\nvertex factor $ig\\gamma^a$. For an electron, $g=-e_{\\rm n}$, so the vertex is\n$-ie_{\\rm n}\\gamma^a$.\n\nFor $\\mu>0$ and off-shell momentum $p$ with $h(p,p)-\\mu^2\\ne0$, set\n$$S_0(p)=\\frac{\\not p+\\mu}{h(p,p)-\\mu^2}.$$\nThe adopted fermion-line coefficient is $iS_0(p)$. In Feynman gauge the\nauxiliary covariant photon-line coefficient is\n$$D^F_{ab}(k)=-\\frac{i h_{ab}}{h(k,k)+i0} =\\frac{i\\eta_{ab}}{h(k,k)+i0}.$$\nThe symbol $+i0$ denotes the selected Feynman boundary-value convention when a\ndistributional line is used; it does not define products of singular loop\ndistributions. The coefficient $D^F_{ab}$ is a calculational tensor factor,\nnot a declaration of four positive physical photon polarization states.\n\nFor the formal matrix realization, use the coefficientwise inverse specified\nby the planned interface [[def-qft-qed-formal-ring]]: if $K_0$ is an invertible\nfinite matrix and $K(z)=K_0+zV$, the formal inverse is the power series in\n$z$; no convergence in $z$ is claimed. The planned off-shell/tree identities\nof [[lem-qft-qed-tree-ward]] fix the $h=-\\eta$ slash and the nonzero-denominator\nconditions. When a time-ordered free distribution is needed, use the actual\ncarrier $H_\\mu$ of [[lem-qft-free-time-ordered-kernel]], for which\n$(\\Box-\\mu^2)H_\\mu=i\\delta_0$ and whose definition has no extra prefactor\n$i$. The distributional use of that carrier is conditional on\n[[def-countable-choice]]; the formal coefficient factors above are adopted\nindependently of that optional distributional identification.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "In the center-of-momentum frame the selected photon momentum is $P$ and $h(P,P)=s>0$. The current QFT-G02 convention [A3] supplies two charge-$-e_n$ vertices and photon factor $-ih_{ab}/(s+i0)$. At this regular point, $\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}(s+i\\epsilon)^{-1}=s^{-1}$, so the scalar denominator is $1/s$. Under the selected-graph normalization [A2], the remaining spin dependence is the stated product of the two currents, up to a common phase that vanishes from the modulus square. This calculation uses no internal fermion line or loop product.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A2",
            "A3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Summing the final spin labels and averaging the four initial pairs, [F3] reduces the squared currents to $$ L_e^{ab}=\\frac14\\operatorname{Tr}[(\\not p_2-m)\\gamma^a(\\not p_1+m)\\gamma^b],\\qquad L_{\\mu,ab}=\\operatorname{Tr}[(\\not k_1+M)\\gamma_a(\\not k_2-M)\\gamma_b]. $$ The terms with three gamma matrices vanish. Expanding the remaining terms with the two- and four-gamma traces in [F3] gives $$ L_e^{ab}=p_2^ap_1^b+p_2^bp_1^a-\\frac{s}{2}h^{ab},\\qquad L_{\\mu,ab}=4(k_{1a}k_{2b}+k_{1b}k_{2a}-\\frac{s}{2}h_{ab}), $$ because $p_1\\cdot p_2+m^2=s/2$ and $k_1\\cdot k_2+M^2=s/2$ follow by expanding the two total-momentum squares.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1-3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "The adjoint equations from [F3] give $\\bar v(p_2)\\not p_2=-m\\bar v(p_2)$ and $\\not p_1u(p_1)=mu(p_1)$, so contraction with $P=p_1+p_2$ cancels the two terms. Similarly, $\\bar u(k_1)\\not k_1=M\\bar u(k_1)$ and $\\not k_2v(k_2)=-Mv(k_2)$ cancel in the outgoing current. This proves both displayed current contractions, including when either mass is zero; no general off-shell or loop Ward statement is inferred.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1-4",
          "step": "1.4",
          "claim": "The declared positive-shell scalar inputs, common total and center-of-momentum evaluation meet [F5] exactly; the decay daughters have total $P_0$ and satisfy its strict rest-parent threshold. Apply [F5] to these declared scalar amplitudes and thresholds. Its identical-final factor is $S_f=2$ on the full labeled sphere, while the distinguishable case has $S_f=1$, so the total scattering coefficients have the stated factor of two. The decay result uses the same identical-particle factor and the parent-rest coefficient map. Since $[G_E]=[m_s]=[\\mathsf M_0]=\\mathrm J$ and $[\\hbar]=\\mathrm{J\\,s}$, $[\\Gamma_{\\rm coeff}]=\\mathrm J^2/(\\mathrm{J\\,s\\,J})=\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$; $(\\hbar c)^2/s$ has unit $\\mathrm{m}^2$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "In this chart $P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$ gives $\\mathbf p_2=-\\mathbf p_1$ and $E_1+E_2=\\sqrt{s}$. The equal mass-shell equations and $E_i>0$ imply $E_1=E_2=\\sqrt{s}/2$, hence $|\\mathbf p_1|=\\sqrt{s/4-m^2}=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_e/2>0$; the same argument gives $\\widetilde E_1=\\widetilde E_2=\\sqrt{s}/2$ and $|\\mathbf k_1|=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_\\mu/2>0$. Thus $\\theta$ is defined. If $\\Lambda_1,\\Lambda_2$ both send $P$ to rest, $\\Lambda_2\\Lambda_1^{-1}$ fixes $(1,\\mathbf0)$ and so acts by a proper spatial rotation by [F4]; it preserves the Euclidean dot product, making $\\theta$ independent of the selected chart's residual orientation. Contracting the tensors in 1.2 and using the mass-shell dot products gives $$ L_e^{ab}L_{\\mu,ab}=4\\{2[(p_2\\cdot k_1)(p_1\\cdot k_2)+(p_2\\cdot k_2)(p_1\\cdot k_1)] +s^2-s(p_1\\cdot p_2+k_1\\cdot k_2)\\}. $$ The cross terms with one metric contribute $-s(p_1\\cdot p_2+k_1\\cdot k_2)$, and the metric-metric term contributes $s^2$; together these equal $s(m^2+M^2)$. The mixed dot products are $p_2\\cdot k_1=p_1\\cdot k_2=s/4+|\\mathbf p_1||\\mathbf k_1|\\cos\\theta$ and $p_2\\cdot k_2=p_1\\cdot k_1=s/4-|\\mathbf p_1||\\mathbf k_1|\\cos\\theta$. Substituting and multiplying by $g^4/s^2$ gives the stated spin-averaged squared coefficient.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "The strict threshold gives $\\beta_e,\\beta_\\mu>0$. For distinguishable muon labels, [F4] gives $d\\Phi_2/d\\Omega=\\beta_\\mu/(32\\pi^2)$ and $F=2s\\beta_e$. Inserting the squared coefficient derived in step 2.1 into its map and using $g^4=(4\\pi\\alpha)^2$ yields the displayed differential coefficient. The sphere integrals are $\\int d\\Omega=4\\pi$ and $\\int\\cos^2\\theta\\,d\\Omega=4\\pi/3$ (integrate $2\\pi u^2$ over $-1\\leq u\\leq1$), giving the displayed total coefficient.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "If $\\delta=4m^2/s\\leq1/2$, set $$ B_0=2-\\beta_\\mu^2+\\beta_\\mu^2\\cos^2\\theta,\\qquad Q=1-\\beta_\\mu^2\\cos^2\\theta. $$ Then $B_0\\geq1$ and $0\\leq Q\\leq1$. The ratio of the full differential coefficient to its $m=0$ value is $$ R=\\frac{B_0+\\delta Q}{B_0\\sqrt{1-\\delta}}\\geq1. $$ Moreover $$ R-1=\\frac1{\\sqrt{1-\\delta}}-1+ \\frac{\\delta Q}{B_0\\sqrt{1-\\delta}} \\leq2\\sqrt2\\,\\delta\\leq4\\delta, $$ since $1/\\sqrt{1-\\delta}-1\\leq\\sqrt2\\delta$ and $B_0\\sqrt{1-\\delta}\\geq1/\\sqrt2$. This compares only the two formal tree coefficients.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "The units and formal/empirical types in [F1]–[F2] identify the results as formal coefficients with area or inverse-time units. The adopted interpretation in [A1] gives only the named leading-coefficient reading; neither an exact continuum observable, a measured estimate, nor an exponential survival law follows.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "A1",
            "step 1.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
