    "pthm-qft-example-dirac-magnetic-benchmark": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "post-qft-qed-local-coupling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt a smooth local classical Maxwell–Dirac model on an open region of the\nflat spacetime specified by [[def-qft-framework-units]]. Let $A_{{\\rm SI},a}$\nbe a supplied real $C^\\infty$ electromagnetic covector potential and let\n$\\psi:\\!U\\to\\mathbb C^4$ be a $C^\\infty$ commuting spinor field. The charge\n$q_{\\rm SI}$ and rest mass $m_{\\rm SI}>0$ are model inputs; for the electron\ntake $q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}$. Use the component, metric, Clifford, charge,\nfield-normalization, and unit conventions of\n[[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]], [[def-qft-qed-physical-gauge-quantities]],\n[[def-qft-framework-units]], and the electromagnetic suppliers of\n[[post-qft-free-photon-model]]. In particular,\n$D_a=\\partial_a-iq_{\\rm SI}A_{{\\rm SI},a}/\\hbar\n=\\partial_a-iq_{\\rm n}a_a$ and $\\mu=m_{\\rm SI}c/\\hbar$.\n\nAdopt the local action density in natural normalization\n\n$$\\mathcal L_{\\rm nat} =-\\frac14 f_{ab}f^{ab} +\\frac{i}{2}\\bigl(\\bar\\psi\\gamma^aD_a\\psi -(D_a\\bar\\psi)\\gamma^a\\psi\\bigr)-\\mu\\bar\\psi\\psi,$$\n\nwhere $f_{ab}=\\partial_a a_b-\\partial_ba_a$ on the region where the\npotential is supplied, $\\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$, and\n$D_a\\bar\\psi=\\partial_a\\bar\\psi+iq_{\\rm n}a_a\\bar\\psi$. The physical action\nis $S_{\\rm cl}=\\hbar\\int_U\\mathcal L_{\\rm nat}\\,d^4x$ with $x^0=ct$. For\ncompactly supported local variations this is the adopted classical\nMaxwell–Dirac formulation; its Euler–Lagrange consequences are not postulated\nas a proof in this item.\n\nFor a smooth gauge function $\\chi$ in webers, the adopted local\ntransformation is\n$A_{{\\rm SI},a}\\mapsto A_{{\\rm SI},a}+\\partial_a\\chi$ and\n$\\psi\\mapsto e^{iq_{\\rm SI}\\chi/\\hbar}\\psi$. It leaves $F=dA$ invariant and\nmakes $D_a\\psi$ transform by the same phase. This is the covector form of the\nSI potential rule in [[def-em-gauge-transformation]]. Gauge functions in the\nlocal variation argument are compactly supported; no assertion is made about\nlarge gauge transformations, nontrivial bundles, boundary charges, or\nasymptotic sectors.\n\nThe model adopts the separately specified free Dirac representation of\n[[post-qft-free-field-model]] and the positive free photon representation of\n[[post-qft-free-photon-model]] as the free-field correspondence used to\ndescribe the selected species. This extra correspondence does not quantize\nthe interacting classical action. In particular, this postulate supplies no\ninteracting continuum four-dimensional QED Hilbert space, no globally\nwell-posed coupled PDE, no pointwise operator-valued composite current, no\nscattering or LSZ construction, and no regulator-removal or\nnonperturbative-existence result.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-qed-gauge-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the fixed Minkowski coordinates $x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)=(ct,x^1,x^2,x^3)$, with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, on an open coordinate domain $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ with its standard topology. Each $x^a$ has unit metre. For a smooth scalar component $u:U\\to\\mathbb R$ or $u:U\\to\\mathbb C$, define $\\partial_a u:=\\partial u/\\partial x^a$; thus $\\partial_0=c^{-1}\\partial_t$ at fixed $\\mathbf x$, $\\partial_j=\\partial/\\partial x^j$ for $j=1,2,3$, and each $\\partial_a$ has unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. Raise derivative indices with $\\partial^a:=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_b$. The fields in this definition are smooth maps\n$$\\phi\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R),\\qquad \\mathbf A\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R^3),\\qquad \\psi\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb C^4),\\qquad \\chi\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R).$$\nDefine the lower-potential component maps $A_a:U\\to\\mathbb R$ by $A_0=-\\phi/c$ and $A_j=\\mathbf A_j$; collectively $(A_a)$ is the real covector potential in this chart. Then $F_{ab}:=\\partial_aA_b-\\partial_bA_a$ has smooth real component maps $F_{ab}:U\\to\\mathbb R$. The spinor is an ordinary commuting $\\mathbb C^4$-valued map, not a tuple of Grassmann generators. Its Dirac adjoint is the smooth row-valued map $\\bar\\psi:=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta:U\\to(\\mathbb C^4)^*$, using the fixed matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], so $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$. In fixed SI units, $\\phi$ is in volts, each $A_a$ is in $\\mathrm{T\\,m}$, $\\psi$ is assigned field dimension $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$, and $\\chi$ is in webers; these unit labels accompany the maps and do not identify $q$ with a measured charge. For periodic spatial domains, the same component and unit conventions apply to smooth periodic coordinate lifts, with the quotient domain specified by the item using them.\n\nFor the SI-normalization bookkeeping, let $c>0$ (m s$^{-1}$), $\\hbar>0$ (J s), $\\mu_0>0$ (N A$^{-2}$), a real charge parameter $q$ (C), and a mass parameter $m>0$ (kg) be symbolic inputs; put $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. In the current SI, $c$ and $h$ are defining constants while $\\mu_0$ is experimentally determined; the formula below keeps $\\mu_0$ symbolic and assigns it no numerical value. Use the lower-potential convention\n$$A_a=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A),$$\nwhere $A_a$ has units T m, $\\phi$ is in volts, and $\\mathbf A$ is in T m. Define\n$$S_0=\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c},\\qquad a_a=A_a/S_0,\\qquad f_{ab}=\\partial_aa_b-\\partial_ba_a,$$\n$$\\mu=mc/\\hbar,\\qquad g=q\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}=q/\\sqrt{\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad D_a=\\partial_a-ig a_a.$$\nHere $D_a$ acts on $\\psi\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb C^4)$ by $(D_a\\psi)(x)=\\partial_a\\psi(x)-ig a_a(x)\\psi(x)$, again a smooth map $U\\to\\mathbb C^4$. Thus $a_a$ and $\\mu$ have units m$^{-1}$, $f_{ab}$ has units m$^{-2}$, and $g$ is dimensionless. For $\\psi$ with units m$^{-3/2}$, both $-\\tfrac14 f_{ab}f^{ab}$ and $\\bar\\psi(i\\gamma^aD_a-\\mu)\\psi$ have units m$^{-4}$; their integral over $d^4x$ is dimensionless and represents the action divided by $\\hbar$ in this normalization.\n\nFor the real smooth gauge-parameter map $\\chi:U\\to\\mathbb R$ in webers, put $\\alpha:=\\chi/S_0\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R)$ (dimensionless) and use\n$$A'_a=A_a+\\partial_a\\chi,\\qquad a'_a=a_a+\\partial_a\\alpha,\\qquad \\psi'=e^{ig\\alpha}\\psi.$$\nThe induced covariant derivative is $D'_a=\\partial_a-ig a'_a$. These are formal mathematical conventions for the later conditional deductions. They do not adopt a measured value of $\\mu_0$, identify $q$ with an electron charge, impose charge quantization, or assert that interacting continuum fields exist.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-qed-physical-gauge-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the oriented, time-oriented Minkowski model, length coordinates, metric,\nand SI/natural dictionary of [[def-qft-framework-units]], with\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and $x^0=ct$. Use the gamma matrices,\nDirac adjoint, and effective Clifford metric of\n[[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]]: $\\gamma^0=\\beta$, $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$,\nand $\\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$. Assume Countable Choice\n[[def-countable-choice]] for the mathematical QFT gauge-data supplier; the\nunit conversions below do not themselves spend a choice principle.\n\nIdentify the SI charge and mass symbols $q,m$ in\n[[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]] with $q_{\\rm SI},m_{\\rm SI}$ here. Its natural\ncoupling $g$ is $q_{\\rm n}$ below, and its inverse-length mass $\\mu$ is the\nsame $m_{\\rm SI}c/\\hbar$.\n\nLet $q_{\\rm SI}\\in\\mathbb R$ be a selected charge parameter in coulombs and\n$m_{\\rm SI}>0$ a selected rest-mass parameter in kilograms. They are inputs\nof the chosen model; neither is derived from Maxwell's equations. Set\n$\\mu=m_{\\rm SI}c/\\hbar$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and\n$q_{\\rm n}=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$, which is dimensionless under the\nframework units of [[def-qft-framework-units]]. For an electron use\n$q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}$ and\n$e_{\\rm n}=e_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}>0$, so $q_{\\rm n}=-e_{\\rm n}$.\nUse $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ with the measured current-SI\n$\\mu_0$ of [[def-qft-framework-units]]. These definitions therefore do not\nassign an exact numerical value to $q_{\\rm n}$, $e_{\\rm n}$, or\n$\\varepsilon_0$.\n\nIn a named inertial chart, let $A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}=(\\varphi/c,\\mathbf A)$ be the\nelectromagnetic potential when one is supplied, with components in\n$\\mathrm{T\\,m}$ under [[def-em-si-conventions]] and\n[[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]]. Define the canonical potential and\nfield-strength components by\n\n$$a^\\mu:=\\frac{A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}},\\qquad f^{\\mu\\nu}:=\\frac{F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}}.$$\n\nThey have engineering dimensions $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and $\\mathrm m^{-2}$,\nrespectively. With the metric-lowered potential\n$A_{{\\rm SI},a}=\\eta_{ab}A^b_{\\rm SI}$, the potential component convention is\n$A_{{\\rm SI},0}=-\\varphi/c$ and $A_{{\\rm SI},i}=A_i$. The covariant derivative\nin the selected QED model is\n\n$$D_a:=\\partial_a-iq_{\\rm n}a_a =\\partial_a-\\frac{iq_{\\rm SI}}{\\hbar}A_{{\\rm SI},a},$$\n\nsince $q_{\\rm n}a_a=q_{\\rm SI}A_{{\\rm SI},a}/\\hbar$. Both terms in $D_a$\nhave unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. If the SI gauge function $\\chi$ has unit\n$\\mathrm{Wb}=\\mathrm{T\\,m^2}$, then $q_{\\rm SI}\\chi/\\hbar$ is dimensionless;\nthe corresponding natural gauge function\n$\\lambda=\\chi/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}$ is dimensionless and\n$q_{\\rm n}\\lambda=q_{\\rm SI}\\chi/\\hbar$. These conversions apply to smooth\nlocal gauge transformations; they assert no boundary condition, asymptotic\ncharge sector, or global gauge-fixing result.\n\nIf a separately specified finite regulator introduces Hamiltonian\neigenvalues, they have unit joule; this definition introduces no regulator.\nThe field components and units refer to the selected inertial chart, not to a\nnew observer congruence or a cross-framework identification.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-example-dirac-pauli-square",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $m>0$ be a particle mass in kilograms, $q\\in\\mathbb R$ its charge in coulombs, and $A:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ a smooth spatial vector potential with components in tesla-metres. Use standard Cartesian coordinates $x_i$ measured in metres and write $\\partial_i:=\\partial/\\partial x_i$. Let $\\epsilon_{ijk}$ be the alternating symbol with $\\epsilon_{123}=1$, and sum repeated spatial indices from $1$ to $3$. Define $B_k(x)=\\epsilon_{kij}\\partial_iA_j(x)$ and $\\mathbf B=(B_1,B_2,B_3):\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$; its smooth components are in tesla.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^4)$ be the common invariant test domain. In this item, “common core” refers only to this shared domain of the displayed differential expressions; no operator-theoretic core property for a closure is asserted. Use the dimensionless matrices $\\alpha_i,\\beta,\\Sigma_i=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_i,\\sigma_i)$ of [[lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness]] and $\\hbar,c$ of [[def-qft-example-data]]. On $\\mathcal C$, define\n$$\\pi_i\\psi=-i\\hbar\\partial_i\\psi-qA_i\\psi,\\qquad H_D\\psi=c\\sum_{i=1}^3\\alpha_i\\pi_i\\psi+\\beta mc^2\\psi.$$\nThese maps preserve $\\mathcal C$. The components $\\pi_i$ have units $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$ and $H_D$ has units joules. Define the operator expressions\n$$\\boldsymbol\\pi^2\\psi:=\\sum_{i=1}^3\\pi_i(\\pi_i\\psi),\\qquad (\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B)\\psi:=\\sum_{i=1}^3\\Sigma_iB_i\\psi.$$\nThen, on $\\mathcal C$,\nHere $[\\pi_i,\\pi_j]:=\\pi_i\\circ\\pi_j-\\pi_j\\circ\\pi_i$, and $H_D^2$ denotes composition on $\\mathcal C$. The identities are\n$$[\\pi_i,\\pi_j]=iq\\hbar\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k,\\qquad H_D^2=m^2c^4I_4+c^2(\\boldsymbol\\pi^2-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B).$$\nThe operator $K:=\\boldsymbol\\pi^2-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B$ maps $\\mathcal C$ to itself and has units $(\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}})^2$.\n\nFor commuting formal indeterminates $t,X$ over $\\mathbb R$, define the first-order square-root coefficient $a$ by\n$$(mc^2+taX)^2\\equiv m^2c^4+tc^2X\\pmod{t^2}.$$\nComparing the coefficient of $tX$ gives $a=1/(2m)$; substituting $K$ for $X$ gives the formal first-degree correction $K/(2m)$, with no operator square-root assertion. Its spin term is $-(q/m)\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B$, where $S_i=\\hbar\\Sigma_i/2$, $\\mathbf S=(S_1,S_2,S_3)$, and $(\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B)\\psi:=\\sum_{i=1}^3S_iB_i\\psi$. The magnetic-moment dot product below uses the same componentwise sum convention.\n\nUnder the coefficient convention $H_{\\mathrm{mag}}=-\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\mathrm{mag}}\\cdot\\mathbf B$ and $\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\mathrm{mag}}=g_s(q/(2m))\\mathbf S$, equality of the formal coefficients as a linear function of $q$ gives $g_s=2$ for the minimal tree-level term. Here $H_{\\mathrm{mag}}$ has units joules and $\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\mathrm{mag}}$ has units $\\mathrm{A\\,m^2}$. No magnetic self-adjoint closure, full spectrum, operator square-root theorem, radiative correction, or empirical prediction is asserted.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-qft-dirac-projections",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], the mathematical scalar shell conventions of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and the second-variable-linear Hilbert pairing of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. In the fixed inertial coordinates $x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] on the affine Minkowski model [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]], $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$; these coordinates have length units under [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. The parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ and momentum coordinate $\\mathbf k$ have inverse-length units, and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ has the same units. The parameter is not identified here with a physical rest mass. Matrix $h_D$ has inverse-length units; the dimensionless spin matrices have no physical unit assignment in this mathematical item.\n\nUse the Pauli matrices $\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and $\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$. On $\\mathbb C^4=\\mathbb C^2\\oplus\\mathbb C^2$, define\n$$\\beta=\\begin{pmatrix}I_2&0\\\\0&-I_2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\alpha_j=\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\sigma_j\\\\\\sigma_j&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\gamma^0=\\beta,\\qquad \\gamma^j=\\beta\\alpha_j.$$\nFor a spinor column $z\\in\\mathbb C^4$, its Dirac adjoint is $\\bar z=z^\\dagger\\beta$. The Clifford contraction is $\\Gamma(p):=\\gamma^0p^0-\\sum_{j=1}^3\\gamma^jp_j$; this contraction uses the effective matrix $-\\eta$ in the relation $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$.\n\nLet $\\mathcal H_D$ be the space of measurable functions $u:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb C^4$, modulo equality almost everywhere, with\n$$\\|u\\|^2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger u(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}<\\infty,$$\nand pairing $\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\int u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\pi)^3$. Define the measurable matrix $h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\sum_j\\alpha_j k_j+\\beta\\mu$ and its maximal multiplication operator with domain $D(h_D)=\\{u\\in\\mathcal H_D:h_Du\\in\\mathcal H_D\\}$. For $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix fields\n$$P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)=\\frac12\\left(I_4\\pm\\frac{h_D(\\mathbf k)}{\\omega(\\mathbf k)}\\right).$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$ at $\\mathbf k=0$, set $P_+(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(I_2,0)$ and $P_-(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(0,I_2)$. These are complementary rank-two orthogonal projections; the value at this Lebesgue-null point does not change their multiplication operators. Write $M_{P_\\pm}$ for their maximal multiplication operators and $\\mathcal H_\\pm=\\operatorname{ran}M_{P_\\pm}$. The companion matrix lemma on this page proves these are bounded orthogonal projections with closed ranges. The conjugate Hilbert space $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$ has vectors $\\bar u$, scalar multiplication $z\\bar u=\\overline{\\bar z u}$, and pairing $\\langle\\bar u,\\bar v\\rangle=\\langle v,u\\rangle$.\n\nUse the sine and cosine definitions of [[def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series]]. For a length-coordinate increment $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix multiplier\n$$W_D(t,\\mathbf k)=\\cos(\\omega t)I_4+i\\frac{\\sin(\\omega t)}{\\omega}h_D(\\mathbf k).$$\nAt the massless apex set $W_D(t,0)=I_4$. Its unitarity, group law and generator identity are proved by the companion matrix lemma on this page.\n\nThe symbols $P_+$ and $P_-$ designate the positive- and negative-energy matrix formulas for this fixed free Dirac operator. This is purely mathematical data; no physical spin-statistics postulate or empirical assertion is included.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-qft-example-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "These are conventions for constructed and formal calculations in this page pair. They are not empirical claims, physical postulates, or existence claims about an interacting continuum theory.\n\nWork on a nonempty open coordinate region $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$, with coordinates $x^0=ct,x^1,x^2,x^3$ in metres and $\\partial_a=\\partial/\\partial x^a$ for $a\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$. Use the geometric spacetime metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and the time coordinate $x^0=ct$ in metres. For Clifford algebra and momentum contraction use the separate algebraic metric $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$; a slash or a square of an energy four-vector uses $h$, never $\\eta$.\n\nA physical energy four-vector is $p=(E,c\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys})$, with each component in joules. Write $M=mc^2$ for rest energy in joules and $p^2=h(p,p)=E^2-c^2|\\mathbf p_{\\rm phys}|^2$. For two such vectors, $s=(p_1+p_2)^2$ is in $\\mathrm J^2$. Natural momentum coordinates are the energy-vector components divided by $\\hbar c$, in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$; they are not substituted into SI formulas without restoring this factor. Here $m\\ge0$ is a mass parameter in kilograms, so $\\mu=mc/\\hbar$ is in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. The natural-unit field convention below concerns smooth test fields, not pointwise quantum operators. A real scalar field is a map $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R)$; a Dirac test field is $\\Psi\\in C_c^\\infty(U;\\mathbb C^4)$. Fix dimensionless matrices $\\gamma^a\\in M_4(\\mathbb C)$ satisfying $\\gamma^a\\gamma^b+\\gamma^b\\gamma^a=2h^{ab}I_4$, and put $\\overline\\Psi=\\Psi^\\dagger\\gamma^0$. Adopt dimensionless action normalizations with kinetic terms $\\tfrac12\\int_U h^{ab}(\\partial_a\\varphi)(\\partial_b\\varphi)\\,d^4x$ and $\\int_U i\\overline\\Psi\\gamma^a\\partial_a\\Psi\\,d^4x$, summing repeated indices from $0$ to $3$. The coefficients are dimensionless and introduce no additional length scale. These conventions assign $[\\varphi]=\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and $[\\Psi]=\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$: the integrands have length degrees $2[\\varphi]-2$ and $2[\\Psi]-1$, while $d^4x$ has degree $4$. No dynamics or continuum existence assertion is adopted.\n\nTake SI $e>0$ in coulombs, $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, $c>0$ in metres per second, $\\mu_0>0$ in $\\mathrm{H\\,m^{-1}}=\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,C^{-2}}$, and $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ in $\\mathrm{F\\,m^{-1}}$. Define\n$$\\alpha=\\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad g=e\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\alpha}>0.$$\nHere $g$ is a dimensionless coupling. It is not the metric and not a gyromagnetic factor.\n\nFor gauge notation take $\\phi\\in C^1(U;\\mathbb R)$ in volts and $\\mathbf A\\in C^1(U;\\mathbb R^3)$ in tesla-metres. Write $A_{\\rm lower}=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A)$ for the components of the real one-form $A=\\sum_{a=0}^3 A_a\\,dx^a$, so every $A_a$ is in tesla-metres. Take $\\chi\\in C^2(U;\\mathbb R)$ in webers and a charged test field $\\psi\\in C^1(U;\\mathbb C^N)$, where $N\\ge1$ is fixed. With a constant real charge parameter $q$ in coulombs, define $D_a\\psi=\\partial_a\\psi-iqA_a\\psi/\\hbar$ componentwise. The transformed fields are again $C^1$, with $d\\chi=\\sum_a(\\partial_a\\chi)dx^a$. In operator notation the conventions are\n$$D_a=\\partial_a-\\frac{iqA_a}{\\hbar},\\qquad \\psi\\mapsto e^{iq\\chi/\\hbar}\\psi,\\qquad A\\mapsto A+d\\chi.$$\nFor the electron parameter use $q=-e$. The equivalent natural-unit coordinates are $A_{\\rm nat}=A_{\\rm SI}/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}$ and $q_{\\rm nat}=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$.\n\nFinite-matrix examples act on $\\mathbb C^d$ with the operator norm. For Hilbert spaces, use the first-variable-linear pairing $(u,v)_1=v^\\dagger u$; a displayed coordinate contraction $u^\\dagger v$ is second-variable-linear and equals $\\overline{(u,v)_1}$. Thus norms, traces, and modulus-squared amplitudes agree under conversion, although the unsquared pairing is conjugated. No amplitude is assigned a probability by these conventions.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-qft-framework-units",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix the oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski spacetime and an inertial\nchart of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use signature\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, length coordinates\n$x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres, and coordinate time $t$ in seconds as in\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Greek spacetime indices run\nfrom $0$ to $3$; spatial indices run from $1$ to $3$ and are contracted with\nthe Euclidean spatial metric. The coordinates $x^a$ have unit metre and\n$\\partial_a:=\\partial/\\partial x^a$ has unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$.\n\nThe positive constants $c$, $h$ and $e_{\\rm SI}$ have the exact SI defining\nvalues; write $\\hbar:=h/(2\\pi)$. The magnetic constant $\\mu_0>0$ has SI unit\n$\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$ and is experimentally determined under the current SI;\nno exact numerical value or current estimate for it is adopted. Put\n$\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ when that symbol is used. These are SI\nnormalization inputs, not empirical claims about a selected QFT model.\n\nFor a species with rest mass $m_{\\rm rest,SI}>0$ in kilograms define its\ninverse-length mass parameter\n\n$$\\mu:=\\frac{m_{\\rm rest,SI}c}{\\hbar}\\quad[\\mathrm m^{-1}].$$\n\nFor a spatial wave vector $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, with each component in\n$\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and the Euclidean norm, define\n$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k):=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}\\in\\mathbb R_{>0}$, with\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. Here $\\omega_\\mu$ is the temporal wave number, not the\nSI angular frequency; the corresponding SI angular frequency is\n$c\\omega_\\mu$. Energy and momentum are $E=\\hbar c\\omega_\\mu$ in joules and\n$\\mathbf p=\\hbar\\mathbf k$ in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$. With\n$p=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k)\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)\\in\\mathbb R^4$, the phase convention is\n\n$$p\\mathbin{\\circ}x:=\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\n\nFor a selected local action represented on a chart region\n$\\mathcal U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ by\n$$\\frac{S}{\\hbar}=\\int_{\\mathcal U}\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{nat}}(x)\\,d^4x,\\quad d^4x:=dx^0dx^1dx^2dx^3,$$\nthe natural Lagrangian density $\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{nat}}$ has unit\n$\\mathrm m^{-4}$. Since $x^0=ct$, the corresponding SI Lagrangian density is\n$\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{SI}}:=\\hbar c\\,\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{nat}}$ and has unit\n$\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$. This unit conversion alone does not identify a\nLagrangian density with the model's Hamiltonian energy density.\n\nFor canonical engineering dimensions in this four-dimensional normalization,\nuse the formal field symbols $\\phi_{\\rm n}$ and $\\psi_{\\rm n}$ in the selected\nscalar and Dirac kinetic terms, assigning them length degrees $-1$ and\n$-3/2$, respectively. These are the dimensions fixed by those free kinetic\nterms, not exact scaling dimensions of interacting fields: anomalous\ndimensions, when present, are separate quantities. These symbols do not\nassert pointwise field maps or operator fields; a model supplies its field\nspace and regularity. Let $A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}=(\\varphi/c,\\mathbf A)$ and\n$F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}$ use the component convention of\n[[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], namely\n$F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, with\n$F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$ only when a potential is\nsupplied. Define the normalized photon potential and field strength by\n\n$$a^\\mu:=\\frac{A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}},\\qquad f^{\\mu\\nu}:=\\frac{F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}}.$$\n\nThey have length degrees $-1$ and $-2$, respectively. The EM supplier is used\nonly for SI units and tensor components: no classical Maxwell equation,\nLorentz-force law, or classical-to-quantum identification is adopted here.\n\nFor any separately declared SI charge parameter $q_{\\rm SI}$ in coulombs put\n\n$$q_{\\rm n}:=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}.$$\n\nIt is dimensionless. For the positive exact SI elementary charge use\n$e_{\\rm n}:=e_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$ and define the rationalized\ndimensionless coupling $\\alpha:=e_{\\rm n}^2/(4\\pi)$. Since $\\mu_0$ is\nmeasured, these formulas do not assign an exact numerical value to\n$e_{\\rm n}$ or $\\alpha$. If the model uses minimal coupling, its convention is\n$D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu-iq_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$; for an electron with\n$q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}$ this reads $D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+ie_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$.\nBare, renormalized-at-scale, and measured effective charges are separately\nlabelled model parameters; applying the conversion to one label does not\nidentify it with either of the others or specify a running law.\n\nIn a supplied four-component spinor representation, let\n$\\gamma^a\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(\\mathbb C^4)$, $a=0,1,2,3$, be\ndimensionless linear maps satisfying\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}:=\\gamma^a\\gamma^b+\\gamma^b\\gamma^a\n=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$, where $I_4$ is the identity map. Set $\\beta:=\\gamma^0$\nand $\\alpha^i:=\\beta\\gamma^i$; then $\\beta^2=I_4$ and\n$\\gamma^i=\\beta\\alpha^i$. For a supplied four-component spinor $\\psi$ write\n$\\bar\\psi:=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$. The effective Clifford metric is $-\\eta$.\nA supplied free Dirac model may use\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0$ for its model-supplied spinor field\n$\\psi$; that model supplies its field domain and regularity. The phase and\nClifford choices are conventions; this item proves no representation or field\nequation.\n\nIf a selected model supplies a complex Hilbert space $\\mathscr H$ as in\n[[def-hilbert-space]], a complex-linear Hamiltonian operator\n$H:D(H)\\to\\mathscr H$ with model-specified linear domain $D(H)\\subseteq\\mathscr H$\nand SI energy unit joules, and an evolution family $U(t):\\mathscr H\\to\\mathscr H$\nfor $t\\in I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ in seconds, the convention is\n$U(t)=\\exp(-itH/\\hbar)$. Each $U(t)$ is a surjective complex-linear map\nsatisfying $\\langle U(t)\\xi,U(t)\\zeta\\rangle=\\langle\\xi,\\zeta\\rangle$\nfor all $\\xi,\\zeta\\in\\mathscr H$; write $U(t)^*:=U(t)^{-1}$. The exponent\nis dimensionless because $\\hbar$ has unit $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. For any\nmodel-supplied complex-linear operator $O:D(O)\\to\\mathscr H$, with specified\nlinear domain $D(O)\\subseteq\\mathscr H$, define its Heisenberg conjugate by\n$$O_H(t):=U(t)^*O\\,U(t),\\quad\nD(O_H(t)):=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathscr H:U(t)\\xi\\in D(O)\\}.$$\nThe model supplies these domains and the evolution; this dictionary constructs\nno Hamiltonian, evolution family, or field representation. Where a free-field\nmodule uses an inner product linear in its second variable, convert to the\nrepository's first-variable-linear pairing by\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_{\\rm FF}=(v,u)_{\\rm root}$.\n\nFor precision about domains, let $\\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space with\ninner product linear in its first variable and let\n$\\mathscr D\\subseteq\\mathcal H$ be a dense complex linear subspace. A **weak operator-valued distribution\non the common domain $\\mathscr D$** means a complex-linear assignment\n$g\\mapsto\\Phi(g)$ for $g\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)$, where each\n$\\Phi(g):\\mathscr D\\to\\mathcal H$ is linear and, for every\n$u,v\\in\\mathscr D$, the scalar map\n$g\\mapsto\\langle\\Phi(g)u,v\\rangle$ is a continuous complex-linear functional\non the test-function space with the topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]], hence a distribution as in [[def-distribution]].\nThis definition gives no pointwise field $\\Phi(x)$ and no product\n$\\Phi(x)\\Phi(x)$; a product or extension needs a separate construction. It\nasserts only matrix-element continuity and the stated linear maps; all further\noperator properties, locality, local observable algebras, graded locality,\nand gauge-specific potential behavior must be supplied by a constructed\nmodel. Test-function units can be chosen so a specified smeared observable is\ndimensionless; there is no universal test-function unit asserted here.\n\nPrimitive data for a selected model include its species and rest masses,\ndeclared charge parameters, regulator geometry, couplings, state and any\nconstructed measurement data. Vacuum-subtracted Hamiltonians, regulator\neigenvalues, distributions, correlations and outcome probabilities are\nderived only when their constructions supply the required domains and\nwell-definedness. This dictionary neither constructs an interacting\nfour-dimensional QED Hilbert space nor asserts pointwise field products,\nscattering, an LSZ limit, asymptotic completeness, or removal of a regulator.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-qft-example-observable-types",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the framework-local unit and domain dictionary of\n[[def-qft-framework-units]] and the energy, SI/natural-unit, and finite-matrix\nconventions of [[def-qft-example-data]]. A physical quantity is always typed\nrelative to the selected QFT formulation and its declared model data; its name\nor units alone do not identify it with a quantity in another formulation.\n\nFor the **exact finite-regulator class** used on this page, the state space is\nthe finite-dimensional Hilbert space $\\mathcal H=\\mathbb C^d$ for an integer\n$d\\ge1$. Model inputs include the selected Hermitian Hamiltonian matrices,\ntheir time-dependent Hermitian interaction when present, a dimensionless\ncoupling parameter, an initial state, and an explicitly supplied finite\noutcome measurement. A pure initial state is a vector $\\psi\\in\\mathbb C^d$\nwith $\\|\\psi\\|=1$; a mixed state is a matrix $\\rho=\\rho^*$ with\n$\\langle\\rho v,v\\rangle\\ge0$ for every $v$ and $\\operatorname{tr}\\rho=1$.\nAn exact regulated observable on this finite space is a specified Hermitian\nmatrix $O=O^*$ with a declared physical unit. The matrix and state are model\ndata; the measurement procedure and any physical rule assigning outcomes are\nadditional data. This definition does not say that an operator symbol by\nitself is a detector outcome or probability.\n\nA **finite probability law** on the declared outcome set\n$\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$, $m\\ge1$, is a vector $(p_1,\\ldots,p_m)$ with\n$p_a\\ge0$ and $\\sum_{a=1}^m p_a=1$. It is a mathematical type. Assigning that\nlaw to a particular preparation and measurement is a separate physical\npostulate. A prediction derived from an actually constructed regulated model\nis conditional on those inputs and the adopted rule.\n\nA **formal coefficient record** is a sequence $(c_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ of\nspecified coefficients, sometimes displayed as the symbol\n$\\sum_{n\\ge0}c_n\\lambda^n$. Unless convergence and an evaluation map are\nseparately proved and adopted, the symbol records coefficients only: it is not\nan evaluated function, exact probability, convergent perturbation series, or\nerror bound. A formal coefficient can be assigned a phenomenological meaning\nonly by a separate, model-specific physical adoption with its approximation\nscope stated.\n\nAn **empirical estimate** is a source-reported number obtained by analyzing a\nfinite record of measurement outcomes under the stated preparation, apparatus,\ncalibration, sampling, and analysis conditions. Its reported uncertainty and\nconditions travel with the estimate. It is not the exact model value, and it\ndoes not prove the framework used to interpret it. The 2022 Fan et al. report\nis an example of a source-reported electron magnetic-moment determination;\nthis definition supplies no numerical result, uncertainty reconstruction, or\nnew experimental claim.\n\nPrimitive and derived status is formulation-relative. In a selected regulated\nmodel, the regulator, Hamiltonian data, coupling, initial state, and supplied\nmeasurement are declared inputs; an evolution, amplitude, or probability law\nis derived only when the construction and adopted rule establish it. In a\nformal calculation, the specified masses, couplings, coefficient rules, and\nsubtraction prescription are inputs, while its listed coefficients are\nderived within that formal calculation. An empirical estimate is a reported\ndata-analysis result, not a primitive model parameter or a theorem.\n\nThe following units are examples of types, not identifications of quantities:\na cross section $\\sigma$ is area-valued with SI unit $\\mathrm{m^2}$; a rate\n$\\Gamma$ is inverse-time-valued with unit $\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$; an energy width\n$\\Gamma_E$ has unit joule. Only a separately stated convention may identify\n$\\Gamma_E=\\hbar\\Gamma$. The fine-structure parameter $\\alpha$, a dimensionless\ncoupling $g$, and a gyromagnetic factor $g_s$ are dimensionless but have\ndifferent physical meanings. The name “width,” a common symbol, or matching\ndimensions does not supply an exponential-decay law or an equality between\nthese parameters.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-qft-qed-local-coupling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt a smooth local classical Maxwell–Dirac model on an open region of the\nflat spacetime specified by [[def-qft-framework-units]]. Let $A_{{\\rm SI},a}$\nbe a supplied real $C^\\infty$ electromagnetic covector potential and let\n$\\psi:\\!U\\to\\mathbb C^4$ be a $C^\\infty$ commuting spinor field. The charge\n$q_{\\rm SI}$ and rest mass $m_{\\rm SI}>0$ are model inputs; for the electron\ntake $q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}$. Use the component, metric, Clifford, charge,\nfield-normalization, and unit conventions of\n[[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]], [[def-qft-qed-physical-gauge-quantities]],\n[[def-qft-framework-units]], and the electromagnetic suppliers of\n[[post-qft-free-photon-model]]. In particular,\n$D_a=\\partial_a-iq_{\\rm SI}A_{{\\rm SI},a}/\\hbar\n=\\partial_a-iq_{\\rm n}a_a$ and $\\mu=m_{\\rm SI}c/\\hbar$.\n\nAdopt the local action density in natural normalization\n\n$$\\mathcal L_{\\rm nat} =-\\frac14 f_{ab}f^{ab} +\\frac{i}{2}\\bigl(\\bar\\psi\\gamma^aD_a\\psi -(D_a\\bar\\psi)\\gamma^a\\psi\\bigr)-\\mu\\bar\\psi\\psi,$$\n\nwhere $f_{ab}=\\partial_a a_b-\\partial_ba_a$ on the region where the\npotential is supplied, $\\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$, and\n$D_a\\bar\\psi=\\partial_a\\bar\\psi+iq_{\\rm n}a_a\\bar\\psi$. The physical action\nis $S_{\\rm cl}=\\hbar\\int_U\\mathcal L_{\\rm nat}\\,d^4x$ with $x^0=ct$. For\ncompactly supported local variations this is the adopted classical\nMaxwell–Dirac formulation; its Euler–Lagrange consequences are not postulated\nas a proof in this item.\n\nFor a smooth gauge function $\\chi$ in webers, the adopted local\ntransformation is\n$A_{{\\rm SI},a}\\mapsto A_{{\\rm SI},a}+\\partial_a\\chi$ and\n$\\psi\\mapsto e^{iq_{\\rm SI}\\chi/\\hbar}\\psi$. It leaves $F=dA$ invariant and\nmakes $D_a\\psi$ transform by the same phase. This is the covector form of the\nSI potential rule in [[def-em-gauge-transformation]]. Gauge functions in the\nlocal variation argument are compactly supported; no assertion is made about\nlarge gauge transformations, nontrivial bundles, boundary charges, or\nasymptotic sectors.\n\nThe model adopts the separately specified free Dirac representation of\n[[post-qft-free-field-model]] and the positive free photon representation of\n[[post-qft-free-photon-model]] as the free-field correspondence used to\ndescribe the selected species. This extra correspondence does not quantize\nthe interacting classical action. In particular, this postulate supplies no\ninteracting continuum four-dimensional QED Hilbert space, no globally\nwell-posed coupled PDE, no pointwise operator-valued composite current, no\nscattering or LSZ construction, and no regulator-removal or\nnonperturbative-existence result.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
