      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "post-qft-qed-local-coupling",
          "carrier": "items/post-qft-qed-local-coupling.md",
          "status": "Current batch-52 draft action postulate inspected for the stated smooth-field density and conversions.",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "exact_statement": "Adopt the local Maxwell-Dirac action density in natural normalization $-\\frac14f_{ab}f^{ab}+\\frac i2(\\bar\\psi\\gamma^aD_a\\psi-(D_a\\bar\\psi)\\gamma^a\\psi)-\\mu\\bar\\psi\\psi$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Smooth real potential, commuting spinor, SI/natural conversion and compactly supported variations; the Euler-Lagrange consequence is left to a consuming proof.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft physical postulate with no Proof section; no independent acceptance.",
          "consumer_use": "Supplies the adopted action."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qft-qed-gauge-data",
          "carrier": "items/def-qft-qed-gauge-data.md",
          "status": "Current batch-44 draft definition and unit/gauge proof inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Use smooth fields on Minkowski coordinate space with $x^0=ct$ and $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Components of $x$ are measured in metres. Fix the Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], so $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$ and $\\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$.\n\nFor the SI-normalization bookkeeping, let $c>0$ (m s$^{-1}$), $\\hbar>0$ (J s), $\\mu_0>0$ (N A$^{-2}$), a real charge parameter $q$ (C), and a mass parameter $m>0$ (kg) be symbolic inputs; put $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. In the current SI, $c$ and $h$ are defining constants while $\\mu_0$ is experimentally determined; the formula below keeps $\\mu_0$ symbolic and assigns it no numerical value. Use the lower-potential convention\n$$A_a=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A),\\qquad F_{ab}=\\partial_aA_b-\\partial_bA_a,$$\nwhere $A_a$ has units T m, $\\phi$ is in volts, and $\\mathbf A$ is in T m. Define\n$$S_0=\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c},\\qquad a_a=A_a/S_0,\\qquad f_{ab}=\\partial_aa_b-\\partial_ba_a,$$\n$$\\mu=mc/\\hbar,\\qquad g=q\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}=q/\\sqrt{\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad D_a=\\partial_a-ig a_a.$$\nThus $a_a$ and $\\mu$ have units m$^{-1}$, $f_{ab}$ has units m$^{-2}$, and $g$ is dimensionless. For $\\psi$ with units m$^{-3/2}$, both $-\\tfrac14 f_{ab}f^{ab}$ and $\\bar\\psi(i\\gamma^aD_a-\\mu)\\psi$ have units m$^{-4}$; their integral over $d^4x$ is dimensionless and represents the action divided by $\\hbar$ in this normalization.\n\nFor a real smooth gauge parameter $\\chi$ in webers, put $\\alpha=\\chi/S_0$ (dimensionless) and use\n$$A'_a=A_a+\\partial_a\\chi,\\qquad a'_a=a_a+\\partial_a\\alpha,\\qquad \\psi'=e^{ig\\alpha}\\psi.$$\nThe induced covariant derivative is $D'_a=\\partial_a-ig a'_a$. These are formal mathematical conventions for the later conditional deductions. They do not adopt a measured value of $\\mu_0$, identify $q$ with an electron charge, impose charge quantization, or assert that interacting continuum fields exist.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Lower-potential convention A0=-phi/c, x0=ct, real charge and AC_omega.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft definition with local proof; no independent approval.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qft-dirac-projections",
          "carrier": "items/def-qft-dirac-projections.md",
          "status": "Current draft mathematical definition inspected for its explicit finite matrices.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], the mathematical scalar shell conventions of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and the second-variable-linear Hilbert pairing of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. In the fixed inertial coordinates $x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] on the affine Minkowski model [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]], $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$; these coordinates have length units under [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. The parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ and momentum coordinate $\\mathbf k$ have inverse-length units, and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ has the same units. The parameter is not identified here with a physical rest mass. Matrix $h_D$ has inverse-length units; the dimensionless spin matrices have no physical unit assignment in this mathematical item.\n\nUse the Pauli matrices $\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and $\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$. On $\\mathbb C^4=\\mathbb C^2\\oplus\\mathbb C^2$, define\n$$\\beta=\\begin{pmatrix}I_2&0\\\\0&-I_2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\alpha_j=\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\sigma_j\\\\\\sigma_j&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\gamma^0=\\beta,\\qquad \\gamma^j=\\beta\\alpha_j.$$\nFor a spinor column $z\\in\\mathbb C^4$, its Dirac adjoint is $\\bar z=z^\\dagger\\beta$. The Clifford contraction is $\\Gamma(p):=\\gamma^0p^0-\\sum_{j=1}^3\\gamma^jp_j$; this contraction uses the effective matrix $-\\eta$ in the relation $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$.\n\nLet $\\mathcal H_D$ be the space of measurable functions $u:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb C^4$, modulo equality almost everywhere, with\n$$\\|u\\|^2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger u(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}<\\infty,$$\nand pairing $\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\int u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\pi)^3$. Define the measurable matrix $h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\sum_j\\alpha_j k_j+\\beta\\mu$ and its maximal multiplication operator with domain $D(h_D)=\\{u\\in\\mathcal H_D:h_Du\\in\\mathcal H_D\\}$. For $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix fields\n$$P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)=\\frac12\\left(I_4\\pm\\frac{h_D(\\mathbf k)}{\\omega(\\mathbf k)}\\right).$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$ at $\\mathbf k=0$, set $P_+(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(I_2,0)$ and $P_-(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(0,I_2)$. These are complementary rank-two orthogonal projections; the value at this Lebesgue-null point does not change their multiplication operators. Write $M_{P_\\pm}$ for their maximal multiplication operators and $\\mathcal H_\\pm=\\operatorname{ran}M_{P_\\pm}$. The companion matrix lemma on this page proves these are bounded orthogonal projections with closed ranges. The conjugate Hilbert space $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$ has vectors $\\bar u$, scalar multiplication $z\\bar u=\\overline{\\bar z u}$, and pairing $\\langle\\bar u,\\bar v\\rangle=\\langle v,u\\rangle$.\n\nUse the sine and cosine definitions of [[def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series]]. For a length-coordinate increment $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix multiplier\n$$W_D(t,\\mathbf k)=\\cos(\\omega t)I_4+i\\frac{\\sin(\\omega t)}{\\omega}h_D(\\mathbf k).$$\nAt the massless apex set $W_D(t,0)=I_4$. Its unitarity, group law and generator identity are proved by the companion matrix lemma on this page.\n\nThe symbols $P_+$ and $P_-$ designate the positive- and negative-energy matrix formulas for this fixed free Dirac operator. This is purely mathematical data; no physical spin-statistics postulate or empirical assertion is included.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Only the explicit finite matrices are consumed; the broader supplier declares AC_omega and Hilbert/shell/Fock interfaces.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition; finite matrices inspected, global operator conclusions not used or certified.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in step 3.1, step 4.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qft-example-dirac-pauli-square",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qft-example-dirac-pauli-square.md",
          "status": "Current batch-46 mathematical draft and full core proof inspected; its spin-trace supplier inherits the Dirac-definition escalation.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $m>0$ be a particle mass in kilograms, $q\\in\\mathbb R$ its charge in coulombs, and $A:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ a smooth spatial vector potential with components in tesla-metres. Set $B_k=\\epsilon_{kij}\\partial_iA_j$ and define on the common core $\\mathcal C=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^4)$\n$$\\pi_i=-i\\hbar\\partial_i-qA_i,\\qquad H_D=c\\sum_i\\alpha_i\\pi_i+\\beta mc^2,\\qquad \\Sigma_i=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_i,\\sigma_i).$$\nHere the matrices are those of [[lem-qft-example-spin-trace-completeness]] and $\\hbar,c$ have the SI conventions of [[def-qft-example-data]]. These differential maps preserve $\\mathcal C$, and on $\\mathcal C$,\n$$[\\pi_i,\\pi_j]=iq\\hbar\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k,\\qquad H_D^2=m^2c^4I_4+c^2(\\boldsymbol\\pi^2-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B).$$\nIf the formal first-order square-root coefficient is written as $mc^2+K/(2m)$ with $K=\\boldsymbol\\pi^2-q\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma\\cdot\\mathbf B$, then its spin term is $-(q/m)\\mathbf S\\cdot\\mathbf B$, where $\\mathbf S=\\hbar\\boldsymbol\\Sigma/2$. Under the coefficient convention $H_{\\mathrm{mag}}=-\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\mathrm{mag}}\\cdot\\mathbf B$ and $\\boldsymbol\\mu_{\\mathrm{mag}}=g_s(q/(2m))\\mathbf S$, equality of the formal coefficients as a linear function of $q$ gives $g_s=2$ for the minimal tree-level term. No magnetic self-adjoint closure, full spectrum, operator square-root theorem, radiative correction, or empirical prediction is asserted.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "All products remain on C_c^infty; no self-adjoint closure is assumed.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft complete conditional proof; independent acceptance pending.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in step 4.1, step 5.1"
        }
      ],
