          "status": "Published mathematical definition; proof is not applicable.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\mathcal T)$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]). The\n**Borel sigma-algebra of $X$** is the sigma-algebra generated by its open sets:\n\n$$\\mathcal B(X):=\\sigma_X(\\mathcal T).$$\n\nIts members are the **Borel subsets** of $X$. The generated sigma-algebra exists\nand is minimal by [[thm-generated-sigma-algebra-exists-and-is-minimal]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The Borel sigma-algebra is generated by the open sets of the stated topology.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical definition; proof is not applicable.",
          "consumer_use": "Defines the event sets for T and its law."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "carrier": "items/def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical definition; well-definedness is assigned to its justified-by theorem.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix $n \\ge 1$ and let $\\lambda_n^*$ be the Lebesgue outer set function on $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n([[def-lebesgue-outer-measure]]). A set $E \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n**Lebesgue measurable** when\n\n$$\\lambda_n^*(A) \\;=\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\cap E) \\;+\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\setminus E) \\qquad \\text{for every } A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n .$$\n\nThis formula makes sense before any outer-measure theorem is invoked. Under\ncountable choice, [[thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-is-an-outer-measure-agreeing-with-volume]]\nmakes $\\lambda_n^*$ an outer measure, and the displayed condition is then exactly\nCarathéodory measurability in the sense of [[def-caratheodory-measurable-set]].\n\nThe family of Lebesgue measurable sets is written $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$,\nand **Lebesgue measure** is the restriction\n\n$$\\lambda_n \\;:=\\; \\lambda_n^*\\!\\restriction_{\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)} .$$\n\nOn the real line the subscript is dropped and $\\lambda := \\lambda_1$.\n\nThe quantifier over every test set $A$ is part of the condition, and no\nhypothesis on $E$ is imposed before it is tested. That\n$\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a sigma-algebra and that $\\lambda_n$ is a complete\nmeasure on it are not part of this definition: they are proved, under the Axiom\nof Countable Choice, in [[thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure]], which is\nrecorded in this item's `justified_by` because it is a statement about the\nobjects introduced here. Until that theorem the symbols $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$\nand $\\lambda_n$ name a family of sets and a restricted set function, nothing more.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "On R, lambda is Lebesgue outer measure restricted to Lebesgue-measurable sets.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical definition; well-definedness is assigned to its justified-by theorem.",
          "consumer_use": "Supplies the measure used in the assumed density identity."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable",
          "carrier": "items/thm-borel-sets-are-lebesgue-measurable.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical theorem with an audited proof.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $n \\ge 1$ and **assume the Axiom of Countable Choice**\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Then\n\n$$\\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^n) \\;\\subseteq\\; \\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n) :$$\n\nevery Borel subset of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ ([[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]) is Lebesgue\nmeasurable ([[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]]). In\nparticular every open set, every closed set and every countable intersection of\nopen sets is Lebesgue measurable.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Assumes Countable Choice and proves every Borel subset of R^n is Lebesgue measurable.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published mathematical theorem with an audited proof.",
          "consumer_use": "Justifies restricting one-dimensional Lebesgue measure to the Borel event sigma-algebra."
        }
      ],
